3. Complete runnable example
function getUsers(): Promise<string[]> {
  return Promise.resolve(["Rahul", "Priya"]);
}

function getProducts(): Promise<string[]> {
  return Promise.resolve(["Laptop", "Phone"]);
}

async function main(): Promise<void> {
  const [users, products] = await Promise.all([
    getUsers(),
    getProducts()
  ]);

  console.log(users);
  console.log(products);
}

main();


Output:
["Rahul", "Priya"]
["Laptop", "Phone"]


Yahan Promise.resolve() already completed Promise return karta hai. Ye example concept samjhane ke liye hai; real API calls mein network ka wait ho sakta hai.
4. Ek important baat: Error handling
Agar Promise.all() mein koi ek Promise reject ho jaye, toh Promise.all() ka returned Promise reject ho jata hai.
async function main(): Promise<void> {
  try {
    const results = await Promise.all([
      getUsers(),
      getProducts()
    ]);

    console.log(results);
  } catch (error: unknown) {
    console.log("Koi task fail ho gaya");
  }
}


Agar tumhe chahiye ki har task ka success/failure alag se mile, toh Promise.allSettled() useful hota hai.
5. Promise.all() vs Promise.allSettled()
Feature	Promise.all()	Promise.allSettled()
Sab successful	Saare results milte hain	Saare status milte hain
Ek fail hua	Returned Promise reject	Baaki results ka wait bhi karta hai
Best use	Sab results zaroori hain	Har task ka outcome chahiye
